The drawing shows an anthracycline of the daunorubicin type: a tetracyclic aglycone carrying a daunosamine-type sugar. I read it region by region and then matched the stereochemistry to daunorubicin from memory, so I did not derive every centre from the wedges independently.

- **Aromatic ring:** one methoxy group sits next to the ring-fusion carbon that connects to the lower quinone C=O.
- **Quinone and hydroquinone rings:** two C=O groups and two phenolic OH groups, with the lower OH adjacent to the carbon attached to the C–O–sugar carbon.
- **Saturated ring:**
  - one quaternary carbon bearing OH (wedge) and an acetyl group;
  - one CH2;
  - one CH bearing the glycosidic oxygen (wedge).
- **Sugar ring:** a tetrahydropyran with:
  - an anomeric carbon with a hashed bond to the glycosidic oxygen;
  - a CH2;
  - a CH–NH2 (wedge);
  - a CH–OH (wedge);
  - a CH–CH3 (wedge) next to the ring oxygen.
- **Sugar stereochemistry:** the NH2 and OH wedges are cis, which fits daunorubicin rather than the 4'-epimer, epirubicin.

SMILES: C[C@H]1[C@H]([C@H](C[C@@H](O1)O[C@H]2C[C@@](Cc3c2c(c4c(c3O)C(=O)c5cccc(c5C4=O)OC)O)(C(=O)C)O)N)O

The drawing shows a pregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione steroid with these features:

- a 9-fluoro group;
- an 11-hydroxyl;
- a 21-hydroxyl on the C(=O)CH2OH side chain;
- a 16-methyl;
- no hydroxyl at C17, only the side chain.

This is desoximetasone (17-deoxy dexamethasone).

The drawing is flipped relative to the usual steroid orientation, with ring A on the right and the wedges inverted. Reading the wedges and hashes with that in mind gives the usual configuration:

- 9α-fluoro;
- 11β-hydroxyl;
- 16α-methyl;
- β side chain at C17;
- β methyls at C10 and C13.

I derived the stereodescriptors by taking dexamethasone's SMILES and replacing its C17 hydroxyl with a hydrogen. I did not independently re-derive them from the drawing.

SMILES: C[C@@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@@]4([C@]3([C@H](C[C@@]2([C@H]1C(=O)CO)C)O)F)C

The structure breaks into three parts.

**Right side.** A benzene ring carries an ethoxy group (CH3CH2–O) on one carbon. The adjacent ring carbon bears a C(=O)NH–CH2– amide group, giving a 2-ethoxybenzamide.

**Middle.** The CH2 is attached to C4 of an oxazole ring. Going around the ring: O1, C2, N3, C4, C5. C2 carries the aryl group, N3 is double-bonded to C2, and C4 is double-bonded to C5.

**Left side.** A benzene ring is attached to oxazole C2 at one carbon. From that carbon, the ring carbons are:
- the next carbon bears the isopropoxy group (OCH(CH3)2);
- the one after that bears the OCHF2 group.

Reading the drawing, the ring vertices are:
- C1 (600,882), attached to the oxazole;
- C2 (608,762), a CH;
- C3 (506,694), bearing the isopropoxy group;
- C4 (397,748), bearing the OCHF2 group;
- C5 (390,868), a CH;
- C6 (492,936), a CH.

So the ring is 3-isopropoxy-4-(difluoromethoxy)phenyl.

SMILES: CCOc1ccccc1C(=O)NCc1coc(n1)-c1ccc(OC(F)F)c(OC(C)C)c1

This SMILES was read from the drawing by hand; I assigned each @/@@ from the wedge and hash geometry, so a stereo error is possible.

**Aglycone.** It is a spirostanol steroid with the usual four fused rings (A–D) plus the spiroketal. Substituents:
- a 2-OH on ring A and an O-glycoside at C3;
- methyl groups at C10 and C13;
- a 15-OH next to the furan oxygen on C16;
- a C20 methyl;
- a spiro tetrahydropyran carrying the C25 methyl.

**Sugar chain at C3-O.**
1. A hexopyranose (first sugar) is attached through its anomeric carbon to the C3 oxygen. Its C4 oxygen links to the next sugar.
2. A second hexopyranose is attached at that C4 oxygen. Its C3 oxygen carries a pentopyranose, and its C2 oxygen carries a third hexopyranose.
3. The pentopyranose is a xylose-type sugar with no CH2OH.
4. The third hexopyranose carries a fourth hexopyranose on its C3 oxygen.

