This looks like the hexafluoro analogue of calcitriol (falecalcitriol, 26,27-hexafluoro-1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3). I wrote the SMILES from the drawn features, and took the stereodescriptors and the triene E/Z notation from my recollection of the natural calcitriol SMILES rather than deriving them from the wedges.

- **Side chain:** a wedged methyl on a CH, then three CH2 groups, ending in a carbon that carries OH and two CF3 groups.
- **Hydrindane core:**
  - a cyclopentane with a wedged bond to the side-chain carbon;
  - a wedged angular methyl;
  - a hashed ring-fusion H;
  - a six-membered ring leading to an exocyclic C=C.
- **Triene and A-ring:** the exocyclic C=C continues through CH=CH to a cyclohexane bearing an exocyclic =CH2 and two OH groups, one wedged and one hashed.

SMILES: C[C@H](CCCC(O)(C(F)(F)F)C(F)(F)F)[C@H]1CC[C@@H]2[C@@]1(CCC/C2=C\C=C/3\C[C@H](C[C@@H](C3=C)O)O)C

I read the structure as unoprostone isopropyl ester. The SMILES below follows the drawing region by region.

**Ester end.** An isopropyl group is attached through O to a carbonyl carbon, C(=O)O–CH(CH3)2.

**Upper chain.** From the carbonyl, the chain runs CH2, CH2, CH2, then CH=CH, then CH2 into the ring. That makes a hept-5-enoate chain. The double bond is drawn cis (Z).

**Ring.** The cyclopentane has five carbons:
- one carbon bears the upper chain on a bold wedge;
- the next carbon bears an OH on a bold wedge;
- a CH2 follows;
- the next carbon bears an OH on a bold wedge;
- the last carbon bears the lower chain on a hashed bond.

**Lower chain.** From the ring this is CH2, CH2, C(=O), then seven more CH2/CH3 carbons. That gives a 3-oxodecyl group.

**Stereochemistry.** I assigned each ring centre from its wedge or hash, with the implicit H on the opposite side. The ring carbons in SMILES order are `[C@H]`, `[C@@H]`, `[C@@H]`, `[C@@H]`.

SMILES: CC(C)OC(=O)CCC/C=C\C[C@H]1[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](O)[C@@H]1CCC(=O)CCCCCCC

I read the structure as a thiopeptide macrocycle. I did not check the SMILES with software.

**Macrocycle.** A central pyridine carries two thiazoles on adjacent carbons (positions 2 and 3). Going around the ring from the pyridine, the units are:
- thiazole, then a C(=O)–NH link to a threonine-type residue (drawn with a hashed bond to N);
- a C(=O)–NH link to a dehydrobutyrine unit, whose C=CH–CH3 has N and CH3 cis (Z);
- thiazole, then a C(=O)–NH link to a valine-type residue bearing an isopropyl on a wedge;
- thiazole, then a C(=O)–NH link to a second threonine-type residue (hashed N bond);
- thiazole, which closes back onto the pyridine.

**Side chain.** The pyridine's 6-position carries a thiazole–thiazole (bithiazole) unit. This is followed by C(=O)–NH, a second Z-dehydrobutyrine, C(=O)–NH–CH2, and finally a CH(OH)CH3 carbon with a hashed methyl.

**Stereochemistry.** I assigned each stereocentre from the wedge or hash and the drawn geometry:
- both threonine-type residues are drawn the same way as L-threonine;
- the valine-type carbon and the terminal CH(OH)CH3 were assigned from the drawn wedges;
- both dehydrobutyrines are Z.

