The drawing shows an anhydrous sodium tetraborate, Na2B4O7: two Na⁺ counterions and a bicyclic tetraborate dianion with four boron atoms and seven oxygens.

- **Boron atoms:** top, bottom, left and right.
- **Bridging oxygens (five):**
  - top-left O joins the left B and the top B;
  - bottom-left O joins the left B and the bottom B;
  - top-right O joins the top B and the right B;
  - bottom-right O joins the bottom B and the right B;
  - the central O joins the top B and the bottom B.
- **Terminal oxygens (two):** the left B and the right B each carry one O⁻.
- **Counterions:** two separate Na⁺.

Each boron has three oxygen neighbours. The SMILES below was checked against this connectivity atom by atom.

SMILES: [Na+].[Na+].[O-]B1OB2OB([O-])OB(O1)O2
